"""One-time scoped owner improvements; never overwrite their existing operating models."""
from pathlib import Path
import json,re,hashlib
D=Path(__file__).resolve().parent;R=D.parents[2]
U={
'M001':('business-plan-examples','Choose an example with the same sales unit', '''<p>The new <a href="/journal/restaurant-business-plan/">restaurant example</a> uses served covers and an average check:60 covers ×26 days ×$25 = $39,000 revenue. The <a href="/journal/catering-business-plan/">catering plan</a> instead uses50 guests ×$40 = $2,000 per event. Neither count is a table-bill count, and catering delivery/setup costs are not restaurant occupancy costs. Pick the example whose unit and delivery process match your business before copying a financial table.</p><p>The <a href="/journal/marketing-agency-business-plan/">marketing-agency plan</a> follows retainers, approval dependencies and delivery hours. Its four-client example needs80 hours; scope creep to28 hours each raises that to112. The existing IT consulting example retains its support/retainer model. Read the <a href="/journal/business-plan-market-analysis/">market-analysis guide</a> for a dated buyer-evidence register, then use the <a href="/journal/business-plan-executive-summary-example/">executive-summary example</a> to communicate checked assumptions.</p><p>For each adapted example, retain a scenario name, period, currency, source date, sales unit, cost boundary and capacity limit. Replace assumptions from your own evidence rather than treating a sample's numbers as market demand.</p>'''),
'M002':('business-plan-financial-projections-service-example','Compare a project budget with a service forecast carefully', '''<p>A launch project and the ongoing service forecast have different boundaries. The <a href="/tools/project-budget-template/">project-budget worksheet</a> keeps actual cost, remaining commitments and additional forecast separate. In its independent $10,000 example, $4,000 actual +$3,000 remaining committed +$1,500 other forecast = $8,500 expected cost and $1,500 budget headroom. That headroom is not this service forecast's cash balance.</p><p>If $500 of the commitment becomes actual, move it between buckets: $4,500 actual +$2,500 remaining committed +$1,500 other forecast still equals $8,500. Do not count the same purchase twice or automatically deduct an incurred cost as a payment on a different date. Transfer only the appropriate dated cash effect into the service forecast after reviewing its scope.</p><p>The <a href="/journal/marketing-agency-business-plan/">agency plan</a> expands service delivery and approval assumptions. Use the <a href="/tools/staffing-plan-template/">role-capacity worksheet</a> before assuming that forecast revenue can be delivered; the service model does not infer available skills or calendar hours.</p>'''),
'M003':('business-plan-for-a-loan','Explain the funding request without presenting it as approval', '''<p>The <a href="/journal/business-plan-executive-summary-example/">new catering executive summary</a> uses a separate fictional $18,000 target: $9,000 equipment, $3,000 setup/deposits, $2,000 launch working cash and $4,000 retained reserve. Proposed owner funding of $10,000 leaves an $8,000 external request. The retained reserve is not another expense, and the request is not an approved facility. These are different figures from this page's earlier worked example.</p><p>Keep an evidence line for every use, its expected payment date, confirmed funding and any conditional source. A model showing three-event overhead coverage in the <a href="/journal/catering-business-plan/">catering plan</a> excludes financing, tax and owner compensation; it cannot establish debt repayment capacity. Add the actual proposed financing terms and an appropriate downside cash schedule before making a repayment claim.</p><p>Ask the recipient for the actual evidence and format it needs. The <a href="/journal/business-plan-software-comparison/">software decision guide</a> explains why a provider's planning or export features do not certify that a lender accepts a plan.</p>'''),
'M004':('business-plan-guide','Link the evidence, operating model and decision in one version', '''<p>Build the customer evidence with the <a href="/journal/business-plan-market-analysis/">market-analysis example</a>, complete the operating assumptions and then draft the <a href="/journal/business-plan-executive-summary-example/">executive summary</a>. Keep one scenario/version across those sections. A larger national market figure cannot override a local delivery-capacity limit, and an interested prospect is not a paid order.</p><p>Use an industry model that exposes the right constraint: <a href="/journal/restaurant-business-plan/">restaurant covers and service periods</a>, <a href="/journal/catering-business-plan/">catering events and deposit timing</a>, or <a href="/journal/marketing-agency-business-plan/">agency retainers and approval hours</a>. Each has a distinct original base/downside model. The existing broad template remains the place to assemble the narrative; no second generic business-plan generator is needed.</p><p>Before the final copy, reconcile sales units ×price, required hours versus available capacity, contribution versus the full cost boundary, funding uses versus sources and dated cash. Compare an intended software/workflow purchase with the <a href="/tools/cost-benefit-analysis-template/">cost-benefit worksheet</a>; its entered benefits are assumptions, not forecast validation. Save the reviewed base and the changed scenario separately.</p>'''),
'M019':('it-consulting-business-plan-retainer-capacity','Do not offset a specialist shortage with unrelated spare hours', '''<p>The new <a href="/tools/staffing-plan-template/">staffing role-capacity worksheet</a> keeps required and available hours separate by skill pool. In an independent weekly example, support needs30 hours against24 available, while implementation needs12 against18. Both totals equal42 hours, yet support still has a six-hour shortage. Spare implementation hours cover it only after skills, timing and existing commitments are verified.</p><p>Use distinct anonymous role labels and allocate shared people's time once. If one consultant supplies both roles, do not enter their entire availability in each row. The tool does not replace the day-level calendar or this page's client-specific retainer and overage checks.</p><p>The <a href="/journal/marketing-agency-business-plan/">agency plan</a> provides a different retainer model with client approvals and content delivery. Its scope-creep example is useful for comparison, but technical support response obligations need their own evidence and cannot be inferred from an agency package.</p>'''),
'M026':('retail-store-business-plan-example','Reconcile opening-stock cash and the cost of sold units', '''<p>The existing opening purchase of120 units at $18 uses $2,160 cash. Selling60 units at $36 produces $2,160 sales and $1,080 cost for those sold units, leaving60 units with an assumed $1,080 stock cost before returns, damage or other adjustments. Do not deduct the full $2,160 purchase and also the $1,080 sold-unit cost from the same profit calculation. The <a href="/journal/cost-of-goods-sold-example/">COGS guide</a> explains the stock-cost bridge; actual returns and valuation need the appropriate records.</p><p>Keep launch commitments separate in the <a href="/tools/project-budget-template/">project-budget worksheet</a>. A $600 setup order with $200 already recognized as actual cost has $400 remaining committed under that stated basis, not another $600 commitment. Whether its cash was paid on the same dates is a separate question. Do not add this independent order to the earlier boutique forecast without explicitly revising the scenario.</p><p>Use the <a href="/journal/business-plan-market-analysis/">market-analysis method</a> to document size-level demand and substitutes. Its catering prospect figures are an example of research structure, not boutique demand. Retain the existing SKU-level reorder and cash-timing checks before using any apparent margin to approve another purchase.</p>''')}
log=[]
for id,(slug,h,txt) in U.items():
 p=R/'journal'/slug/'index.html';s=p.read_text();marker='business-planning-day2'
 if 'id="'+marker+'"'in s:continue
 old=s;pub=re.findall(r'"datePublished"\s*:\s*"([^"]+)"',s);mods=re.findall(r'"dateModified"\s*:\s*"([^"]+)"',s)
 s=s.replace('</article>',f'<section id="{marker}"><h2>{h}</h2>{txt}</section></article>',1);assert s!=old
 s=re.sub(r'("dateModified"\s*:\s*")[^"]+',r'\g<1>2026-11-19',s);s=re.sub(r'(<meta property="article:modified_time" content=")[^"]+',r'\g<1>2026-11-19',s)
 p.write_text(s);log.append(dict(master_id=id,owner='/journal/'+slug+'/',datePublished=pub,previousDateModified=mods,previous_sha256=hashlib.sha256(old.encode()).hexdigest(),heading=h,decision='Retain existing model and artwork; add distinct original decision check and contextual cluster links'))
if log:(D/'audits.json').write_text(json.dumps(log,indent=2)+'\n')
print('Applied scoped improvements to',len(log),'existing owners')
